        "source-05-quantum-machine-learning--a91f0184eb29"
      ]
    },
    {
      "body": "The dominant themes are scale, noise, verification, and hardware-native adaptation. Random circuits become harder to simulate as qubits and depth grow, but their claims depend on cross-entropy benchmarking, component-error models, and extrapolation from verifiable circuits. Hybrid variational methods respond to short coherence by replacing long coherent evolution with repeated expectation measurements and classical optimization. Quantum-network claims likewise center on preserving coherence across matter-light interfaces, while quantum machine learning turns on whether qBLAS, HHL, qPCA, or quantum-data workflows can avoid data-loading and readout bottlenecks.",
